## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of concurrency in OS caches, race handling, and data integrity under contention.

## Key Concepts
- CAS-based locking and per-block loading state
- Wait queues and wakeups for concurrent readers
- Read-ahead decisions and eviction interactions

## Code Example
```c
// Minimal sketch in C-like pseudo
typedef struct BlockEntry { inode_t *inode; int block_no; void *data; atomic_int loading; atomic_int ready; wait_queue_t wq; } BlockEntry;
BlockEntry *cache_lookup(inode_t*, int); void disk_read_async(inode_t*, int, void*);

void* get_block(inode_t *inode, int block_no) {
  BlockEntry *e = cache_lookup(inode, block_no);
  if (e && atomic_load(&e->ready)) return e->data;
  if (e == NULL) {
    e = allocate_entry(inode, block_no);
    if (atomic_compare_and_swap(&e->loading, 0, 1)) {
      disk_read_async(inode, block_no, e->data);
      atomic_store(&e->ready, 1);
      wake_all(&e->wq);
    } else {
      wait_event(&e->wq, atomic_load(&e->ready) == 1);
    }
  } else {
    wait_event(&e->wq, atomic_load(&e->ready) == 1);
  }
  return e->data;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you verify no duplicate disk reads occur under concurrent requests?
- How would you extend with proactive read-ahead and cache eviction rules?

Why This Is Asked
Tests lock-free data structures, cross-core coordination, and end-to-end IO flow. It gauges ability to design scalable, low-latency paths with correct completion routing.

Key Concepts
- Per-device queues and shared completion path
- Lock-free enqueue/dequeue via CAS
- Correct delivery to originating process
- Backpressure, burst handling, and deadlock avoidance

Code Example
```c
// Pseudo-C: per-device ring enqueue
typedef struct req { void* buf; size_t len; int op; } req_t;
typedef struct ring { _Atomic(int) head, tail; req_t* buf[N]; } ring_t;
int enqueue(ring_t* r, req_t* e){
  int t = atomic_load(&r->tail);
  int next = (t + 1) % N;
  if (next == atomic_load(&r->head)) return -1; // full
  r->buf[t] = e;
  atomic_store(&r->tail, next);
  return 0;
}
req_t* dequeue(ring_t* r){
  int h = atomic_load(&r->head);
  int t = atomic_load(&r->tail);
  if (h == t) return NULL; // empty
  req_t* e = r->buf[h];
  atomic_store(&r->head, (h + 1) % N);
  return e;
}
``` 

Follow-up Questions
- How to scale with multiple devices sharing completion rings?
- How to handle out-of-order completions and per-process cancellation?
- What tests validate fairness under mixed device load?

Why This Is Asked: tests understanding of synchronization primitives, fairness, and memory ordering. Key Concepts: ticket locks, wake queues, avoiding starvation, spurious wakeups, memory barriers. Code Example: see below. Follow-up: discuss adaptation to multicore/NUMA, impact of preemption, and testing strategies.

Code Example:
```c
#include <stdatomic.h>

typedef struct {
  atomic_uint next;
  atomic_uint nowServing;
} ticket_lock_t;

void ticket_lock_init(ticket_lock_t *lk){
  atomic_store(&lk->next, 0);
  atomic_store(&lk->nowServing, 0);
}

unsigned ticket_lock_acquire(ticket_lock_t *lk){
  unsigned ticket = atomic_fetch_add_explicit(&lk->next, 1, memory_order_relaxed);
  while (atomic_load_explicit(&lk->nowServing, memory_order_acquire) != ticket) {
    _mm_pause();
  }
  return ticket;
}

void ticket_lock_release(ticket_lock_t *lk){
  atomic_fetch_add_explicit(&lk->nowServing, 1, memory_order_release);
}
```

Follow-up Questions:
- How would you test fairness under heavy contention?
- How would you extend to multiple locks and prevent deadlock?

